---
<BaseLayout title="LI20 (Yingxiang) — Welcome Fragrance Point" description="Learn how to locate and stimulate LI20 (Yingxiang) — the Welcome Fragrance point. Benefits include nasal congestion relief, sinus pressure, and facial pain support." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points/" }, { label: "LI20 (Yingxiang)", href: "/points/li20-yingxiang-acupressure-point/" }]} datePublished="2026-08-18" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">LI20 (Yingxiang) — Welcome Fragrance</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">LI20, known as Yingxiang or Welcome Fragrance, sits beside the nostrils and is the classic acupressure point for nasal congestion, sinus pressure, and facial pain. Two fingertips pressing gently upward and outward for thirty to sixty seconds is the standard technique.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Large Intestine (LI)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">In the small hollow beside each nostril, where the nose meets the cheek</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Nasal congestion, sinus pressure, allergic rhinitis, facial pressure</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Very easy — beside each nostril, no anatomy to learn</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">30–60 seconds per side</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">A few times daily, as needed</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">LI4 (Hegu)</a>, <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">GB20 (Fengchi)</a>, <a href="/points/yintang-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">Yintang</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Recent nose surgery (including rhinoplasty), or broken skin on or inside the nose (see Precautions)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why LI20 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">LI20 is the face's congestion point. It sits on the Large Intestine meridian, and classical practice reaches for it whenever the nose feels blocked — nasal congestion, sinus pressure, facial pain, and the stuffiness of colds and allergies. Its name, Welcome Fragrance, hints at helping the nose take in breath and scent again.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Because it sits beside the nostrils, LI20 is one of the easiest points on the body to find and press — you can use it at a desk, in a car, or before bed without any preparation. It appears routinely in face and sinus routines alongside Yintang between the brows and points on the hands and neck, which is why it is often the first point people reach for when congestion makes breathing and sleeping uncomfortable.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/li20-yingxiang-location.webp" alt="LI20 (Yingxiang) acupressure point location beside the nostrils" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm mb-6" width="750" height="500" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">LI20 is in the small indentation beside the nostrils, at the side of the nose where it meets the cheek — roughly half a finger width out from the midpoint of the nostril wing, in the nasolabial groove. Press a fingertip into the hollow just outside each nostril; the correct spot feels achy and sits in a slight hollow.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) the hollow sits just outside the nostril where the side of the nose meets the cheek, not on the cheekbone or on the nose itself; (2) pressing upward and outward produces a gentle, achy sensation that may make you want to breathe in deeper; (3) the more congested side of your nose usually has the more sensitive spot.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use LI20 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-forest-200 bg-forest-50 dark:border-forest-900/40 dark:bg-forest-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• Your nose is blocked and you want to breathe more easily</li>
          <li>• Sinus pressure or facial fullness is building during a cold or allergy flare</li>
          <li>• Allergic rhinitis — sneezing, a runny or itchy nose</li>
          <li>• You want a quick, discreet comfort break before sleep or during the day</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You recently had nose surgery, including a rhinoplasty</li>
          <li>• There is broken skin, a cold sore, or a healing wound on or around the nose</li>
          <li>• You have a fever, facial swelling, or pain over the sinuses</li>
          <li>• You are having an active or frequent nosebleed</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: LI20 is a comfort practice for congestion, not a decongestant or a treatment for a sinus infection.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Nasal congestion:</strong> LI20 is the classic point for a blocked nose and opens the nasal passages.</li>
      <li><strong>Sinus pressure:</strong> It is described as useful for easing the pressure of congestion.</li>
      <li><strong>Facial pain:</strong> Alongside Yintang and LI4, it appears routinely in routines for sinus congestion and facial pressure.</li>
      <li><strong>Cold and allergy symptoms:</strong> The stuffiness of colds and seasonal allergies is a traditional target.</li>
      <li><strong>Gentle comfort:</strong> The face guide frames this as a comfort practice rather than a decongestant.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Massage LI20</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Facial work favors a light, precise touch. Keep nails short and never press inside the nostrils or on the eyeballs.</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Place two fingertips on LI20 beside each nostril.</li>
      <li>Press gently upward and outward for thirty to sixty seconds.</li>
      <li>Breathe lightly and evenly while you hold the pressure.</li>
      <li>Build toward a soft, deep ache (the deqi response) with far less force than the legs or feet demand.</li>
      <li>Release slowly, work both sides evenly, and repeat a few times a day.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing too hard, or pressing on the eyeball or inside the nostril. The face is delicate — the target is a soft, deep ache from a light fingertip, and both sides should be worked evenly rather than digging in on one side.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine LI20 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A congestion routine works better than pressing one point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 30–60 seconds each.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Blocked nose / sinus congestion</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">LI20 → <a href="/points/yintang-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">Yintang</a> → <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">LI4</a>, 30–60s each, repeat 2–3 times daily</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Allergic rhinitis flare</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">LI20 → <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">GB20</a> → LI4, gentle holds, plus your usual allergy care</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Congestion with frontal head pressure</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">LI20 → Yintang → GB20, holding each 30–60s</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Stuffy nose before sleep</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">LI20 + Yintang, gentle, with a few slow breaths before lying down</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a fuller program, see the <a href="/body/face-acupressure-points/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">face acupressure points</a> body guide and our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-headache/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">acupressure for headache</a> page for sinus-related head pressure.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">The nasal passages often feel easier within a minute or two of pressing LI20. Relief can be temporary — if the congestion comes from an infection or an allergy, it will return until the underlying cause clears. Do not expect a single press to open the nose for hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">Repeated gentle use a few times a day tends to keep sinus pressure lighter and makes breathing feel less effortful. This is comfort support — it does not replace your usual allergy or cold care, and it cannot clear an infection by itself.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For seasonal allergies, a daily routine through the season works better than occasional pressing. Persistent sinus pain, fever, or congestion that lasts more than about 10 days needs a clinician's review rather than more self-treatment.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Research on facial acupressure is limited, and most support for LI20 comes from traditional practice rather than large clinical trials. Systematic reviews of acupressure for pain and respiratory complaints show generally promising but mixed-quality evidence, and the specific point is rarely isolated in trials — the strongest signals come from broader studies of manual stimulation for congestion and discomfort. You can read a fuller assessment of what this evidence does and does not show on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work/">does acupressure work?</a> page.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">The honest framing is that LI20 is a comfort practice rather than a decongestant — it cannot clear an infection or an allergy by itself. Used alongside usual care, gentle facial pressure can help with stuffiness and sinus pressure, but persistent sinus pain or fever still needs a clinician's review.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["song-2015"]} note="The review above assessed self-acupressure across allergic and respiratory complaints. It is not a study of LI20 in isolation, but LI20 is the point most commonly used for nasal congestion." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Keep pressure light — facial points need far less force than the legs or feet.</li>
      <li>Never press the eyeballs or the inside of the nostrils.</li>
      <li>Skip the point if the area is irritated or after recent facial or nasal surgery, including rhinoplasty.</li>
      <li>Wash your hands before pressing near the nose.</li>
      <li>Persistent sinus pain or fever needs a clinician's review.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>Fever, facial swelling, or pain over the sinuses</li>
        <li>Green or bloody nasal discharge that persists</li>
        <li>Vision changes, double vision, or swelling around the eye</li>
        <li>Congestion that lasts more than about 10 days or keeps coming back</li>
        <li>Frequent or hard-to-stop nosebleeds</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How do I find LI20 on myself?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">LI20 sits in the small indentation beside each nostril, at the side of the nose where it meets the cheek — roughly half a finger width out from the midpoint of the nostril wing. The correct spot feels achy and sits in a slight hollow; tenderness and a depression confirm it.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I press LI20?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Thirty to sixty seconds with two fingertips pressing gently upward and outward is the standard hold, and you can repeat it a few times a day as needed. Facial points take a shorter hold and lighter pressure than the one-to-two-minute standard used on the limbs because the face is delicate.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can LI20 help with allergies and sinus congestion?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">LI20 is the classic point for nasal congestion, sinus pressure, and the stuffiness of colds and allergies, but it is a comfort practice rather than a decongestant — it cannot clear an infection or an allergy by itself. Persistent sinus pain or fever should be reviewed by a clinician.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is LI20 safe during pregnancy?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">The points traditionally avoided during pregnancy are LI4 (Hegu), SP6 (Sanyinjiao), BL60 (Kunlun), and GB21 (Jianjing); LI20 is not among them. Keep pressure gentle and check with your healthcare provider if you have concerns.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor instead?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">See a doctor for a fever, facial swelling, or pain over the sinuses, for congestion that lasts more than about 10 days, or for vision changes or frequent nosebleeds. LI20 is comfort care — sinus infection and structural problems need a clinician's review.</div>
    </details>
    </section>
    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">Related guides: <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-sinus/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">acupressure for sinus pressure</a>.</p>

    <div class="mt-10 text-center">
      <a href="/points/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-forest-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-forest-300">Back to All Points</a>
    </div>
  </article>
